    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00241:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Conversion of Carboxylic Acids into Acid Chlorides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00001",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00001-alt",
              "text": "2, 4, 6-Trimethylbenzoic acid reacts with thionyl chloride in chloroform (trichloromethane) forming 2, 4, 6-trimethylbenzolchloride (ninety percent), H C l, and sulfur dioxide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_001.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "A carboxylic acid reacts with thionyl chloride giving an acid chloride and sulfur dioxide. A chlorosulfite (intermediate) is depicted in parentheses which is formed when chloride ion attacks carbonyl carbon."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00241:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Conversion of Carboxylic Acids into Acid Anhydrides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "The reaction shows the formation of acetic anhydride and water upom heating of two equivalents of acetic acid at eight hundred degrees Celsius."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00003",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00241:title:sect-00003-title",
        "text": "Conversion of Carboxylic Acids into Esters"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "A curly arrow S N 2 mechanism for the reaction of sodium butanoate and methyl iodide, giving methyl butanoate (ninety-seven percent) and sodium iodide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00006",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00006"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00005",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00005-alt",
              "text": "The reversible reaction of benzoic acid with ethanol in the presence of an H C l catalyst giving ethyl benzoate (ninety-one percent) and water."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_006.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00007",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00007"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00006",
          "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00241:caption:fig-00006-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00006-alt",
              "text": "A curly arrow mechanism for Fischer esterification shows the four steps in converting a carboxylic acid to an ester. The steps comprise protonation, nucleophilic attack, proton transfer, and loss of water."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_005.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00008",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00008"
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00009",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00009"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00007",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00007-alt",
              "text": "The reversible reaction shows benzoic acid and methanol in the presence of a hydrochloric acid catalyst giving methyl benzoate and water. The oxygen atoms of methanol and the methoxy group are starred."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_007.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "id": "exam-00001",
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00241:example-title:exam-00001-title",
            "text": "Synthesizing an Ester from an Acid"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00010",
              "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00010"
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00011",
              "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00011",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00241:para-title:para-00011-title",
                "text": "Strategy"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00012",
              "title": {
                "segmentId": "m00241:para-title:para-00012-title",
                "text": "Solution"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00008",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00008-alt",
              "text": "The structure of an ester shows a benzene ring connected to a propoxy carbonyl group. A bromine is attached to the ortho position of the ring."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_008.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00009",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00009-alt",
              "text": "The reaction between o-bromobenzoic acid and 1-propanol using a hydrochloric acid catalyst gives propyl-o-bromobenzoate and water. The bromine atom is ortho to the propoxy carbonyl group in benzene ring."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_009.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
