    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00005",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00241:title:sect-00005-title",
        "text": "Conversion of Carboxylic Acids into Amides"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00015",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00015"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00010",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00010-alt",
              "text": "The structure shows two carbodiimides. The first structure is dicyclohexylcarbodiimide. The second structure is 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-carbodiimide. In the two structures, the centrak carbon atom is double-bonded to two nitrogen atoms."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_dicyclo.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00016",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00016"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00011",
          "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00241:caption:fig-00011-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00011-alt",
              "text": "A curly arrow mechanism shows the four steps in the reaction between a carboxylic acid and dicyclohexylcarbodiimide (D C C) to form an amide and dicyclohexylurea."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_013.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00017",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00017"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00012",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00012-alt",
              "text": "The reaction shows the formation of a dipeptide from the two different amino acids labeled as one and two, in the presence of D C C."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_012.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00006",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00241:title:sect-00006-title",
        "text": "Conversion of Carboxylic Acids into Alcohols"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00018",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00018"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00013",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00013-alt",
              "text": "The reaction shows the conversion of a carboxylic acid to a primary alcohol using lithium aluminum hydride. An aldehyde and an alkoxide ion, enclosed in parentheses, are formed as intermediates."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_014.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00019",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00019"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00014",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00014-alt",
              "text": "The reaction shows the conversion of a carboxylic acid to an aldehyde using hydride ions from lithium aluminum hydride. A carboxylate ion and dianion, enclosed in parentheses, are formed as intermediates."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_015.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00020",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00020"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00015",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00015-alt",
              "text": "The conversion of p-nitrophenylacetic acid with borane in T H F in the first step followed by acid in the second step, gives 2-(p-nitrophenyl)ethanol (ninety-four percent)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_016.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00007",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00241:title:sect-00007-title",
        "text": "Biological Conversions of Carboxylic Acids"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00021",
          "segmentId": "m00241:para:para-00021"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00016",
          "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00241:caption:fig-00016-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00241:alt:fig-00016-alt",
              "text": "A curly arrow mechanism for the reaction of A T P with carboxylate ion to give fatty acyl coenzyme A and adenosine monophosphate."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_03_017.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
